<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Bohtan</h1><p class="st-meta">1384 CE – 1847 CE (approx.) · capital Cizre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish emirate at Cizre on the Tigris, setting of the epic Mem u Zin and seat of Bedir Khan, whose revolt shaped modern Kurdish nationalism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Azizan family, which claimed descent from the Arab general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Jazirat ibn Umar</a> (Cizre) and the Bohtan region on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, their power predating the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After 1514 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> accepted Ottoman suzerainty and kept hereditary rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a> was a centre of Kurdish learning: the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani">Ahmad Khani</a> set his epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-and-zin">Mem u Zin</a> of 1692 at its court. In the 1840s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedir-khan-beg">Bedir Khan Beg</a> extended his power across the region, and his forces massacred Assyrian Christians of Hakkari in 1843 and 1846.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ottoman troops defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedir-khan-beg">Bedir Khan</a> in 1847 and exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>. His descendants, the Bedirxan family, became leading Kurdish nationalists, publishing the first Kurdish newspaper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, in 1898.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1384 CE – 1847 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.24%;width:9.21%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1384 CE – 1847 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cizre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bedir Khan Beg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Botan, Cizre emirate, Azizan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1616&amp;focus=emirate-of-bohtan" aria-label="Emirate of Bohtan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5735 834 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Bohtan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6055 1090L6003 1071M6055 1090L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6003 1071h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6055 1090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6055 1090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cizre. <a href="/atlas?year=1616&amp;focus=emirate-of-bohtan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

